KZ3 patch 1109 (SPU pass 82): the lock-line coherency registry grows in chunks instead of holding 8 contexts.

Patch 1056 kept the reservation-holder registry as one array of 8 atomic slots (SPU_COH_MAX_CTX) so its striped
walkers could read it without the global lock. A 9th holder was not registered: one warning, then that context never
got its lock-line (LR) events and a PUTLLC/GETLLAR wait on it relied on timeouts. 135 of 555 Proton logs under
work/proton print "[spu-coh] more than 8 reserving SPU contexts" (newest spu-29a/b, 2026-10-10; none in the runs
since, which postdate 1052/1084's unregister fixes). Upstream PR #290's test_spu_coherency.c registers 20 ("beyond
eight") and failed on our stack.

Now: a chain of 16-slot chunks, the first static, the others appended with a SEQ_CST CAS on the last chunk's next
(new chunk already holding the context, so publishing it is the same instant as the old slot CAS) and never freed,
so a walker holding a stripe still reads every slot lock-free. Holes left by unregister are reused before a chunk is
added. Out of memory for a chunk aborts (a missed holder loses events, including ABA); never a silent drop.
KZ3_LL_STATS=1 also prints "[spu-coh] registry grew to N slots" at each new chunk. spu_coh_capacity() for tests.
Host tests: tools/spu/test_spu_event_race.sh (test_spu_coh_registry.c: 100 holders all killed, except-self past
the first chunk, hole reuse, 8 threads registering 512 contexts while a notifier walks; also built with ASan/UBSan
and TSan), tools/spu/test_lockline.sh (upstream test_spu_coherency.c beyond-eight case).

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): chunked registry + watch wake.

